{"name":"Gold Plated","hex":"#b0834f","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"gold plated","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Alternative form of gold-plated."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of gold plate"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gold_plated"}],"entries":[{"title":"Gold Plated","blocks":[{"text":"Gold Plated is a 1976 album by the Climax Blues Band. It made No. 56 on the UK Albums Chart and No. 27 on the Billboard Album Chart, becoming their highest-selling album in either region. It also featured their hit \"Couldn't Get It Right\"."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/5/5b/The_Climax_Blues_Band_-_Gold-Plated_%281976%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_Plated","wikidata":"Q17015309"},{"title":"Gold plating","blocks":[{"text":"Gold plating is a method of depositing a thin layer of gold onto the surface of another metal, most often copper or silver, by a chemical or electrochemical (electroplating) process. Plating refers to modern coating methods, such as the ones used in the electronics industry, whereas gilding is the decorative covering of an object with gold, which typically involve more traditional methods and much larger objects."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/The_Sounds_of_Earth_Record_Cover_-_GPN-2000-001978.jpg/330px-The_Sounds_of_Earth_Record_Cover_-_GPN-2000-001978.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_plating","wikidata":"Q3391704"},{"title":"Plating","blocks":[{"text":"Plating is a finishing process in which a metal is deposited on a surface. Plating has been done for hundreds of years; it is also critical for modern technology. Plating is used to decorate objects, for corrosion inhibition, to improve solderability, to harden, to improve wearability, to reduce friction, to improve paint adhesion, to alter conductivity, to improve IR reflectivity, for radiation shielding, and for other purposes. Jewelry typically uses plating to give a silver or gold finish."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/2025-08-01_Barel-plating-method%E3%83%90%E3%83%AC%E3%83%AB%E3%82%81%E3%81%A3%E3%81%8D%EF%BC%88%E3%82%AC%E3%83%A9%E3%82%81%E3%81%A3%E3%81%8D%EF%BC%89KODAMA.jpg/330px-2025-08-01_Barel-plating-method%E3%83%90%E3%83%AC%E3%83%AB%E3%82%81%E3%81%A3%E3%81%8D%EF%BC%88%E3%82%AC%E3%83%A9%E3%82%81%E3%81%A3%E3%81%8D%EF%BC%89KODAMA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plating","wikidata":"Q1193988"},{"title":"Gold","blocks":[{"text":"Gold is a chemical element; its chemical symbol is Au and atomic number 79. In its pure form, it is a bright-metallic-yellow, dense, soft, malleable, and ductile metal. Chemically, gold is a transition metal, a group 11 element, and one of the noble metals. It is one of the least reactive chemical elements, being the second lowest in the reactivity series, with only platinum ranked as less reactive. Gold is solid under standard conditions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Gold-crystals.jpg/330px-Gold-crystals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold","wikidata":"Q897"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Stirling_Engine.jpg/500px-Stirling_Engine.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Stirling_Engine.jpg/500px-Stirling_Engine.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A gold plated desktop Stirling engine","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AStirling_Engine.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.or